{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "The voltage derivative depends on adaptation only through an additive opposing current A that is independent of membrane voltage, so at fixed A and membrane_capacitance the A-to-dV_dt slope is constant and voltage-independent.",
      "child_relation": "Adaptation remains hyperpolarizing but its effective opposing current becomes voltage-dependent, so the influence of A on dV_dt strengthens with depolarization and weakens near rest, creating a V-by-A interaction in the target mapping.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "V",
        "A",
        "resting_voltage",
        "membrane_capacitance"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "For matched adaptation magnitude A under the same applied_current and membrane_capacitance, the reduction in dV_dt is larger at more depolarized subthreshold voltages than near rest; equivalently, dV_dt versus V curves separate more strongly across A at elevated V."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "depolarization",
        "expression": "(V - resting_voltage + Abs(V - resting_voltage))/2",
        "role": "nonnegative distance above rest where adaptation-gated conductance effects are recruited"
      },
      {
        "name": "adapt_load",
        "expression": "A/membrane_capacitance",
        "role": "baseline adaptation-induced slowing per unit capacitance in the parent mapping"
      },
      {
        "name": "rectified_leak_drive",
        "expression": "leak_conductance*(1 + leak_rectification_strength*(V - resting_voltage)**2)*(V - resting_voltage)/membrane_capacitance",
        "role": "existing passive restorative voltage-dependent drive against which voltage-dependent adaptation would interact"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable during sustained subthreshold depolarization where V is several millivolts above resting_voltage and adaptation has had time to build to a nonzero level; the effect is weak when A≈0 or V is near/below rest.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, increasing depolarization already recruits larger A and leak rectification; in holdout, the same subthreshold regime extends to stronger sustained depolarization where equal A values produce a larger suppressive effect on dV_dt than the parent's voltage-independent additive adaptation can capture with one shared parameter set.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law when the voltage dependence of adaptation current is taken to zero, yielding a purely additive subtraction of A from the numerator of dV_dt.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model treats the adaptation variable as an ideal current source whose instantaneous effect on voltage is independent of membrane voltage, although many subthreshold adaptation mechanisms act more like voltage-dependent outward conductances with a driving force relative to a reversal potential.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Adaptation enters the membrane equation as a voltage-independent hyperpolarizing current -A.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Subthreshold adaptation channels often generate outward current proportional to both a slow activation variable and the membrane driving force, making adaptation efficacy increase with depolarization even before spiking.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "V",
      "A",
      "resting_voltage",
      "membrane_capacitance"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the ideal-current assumption for adaptation rather than add an unrelated process. Hold applied_current, leak_conductance, adaptation kinetics, and baseline_membrane_capacitance fixed, and compare trajectories or sampled states with the same nonzero A but different subthreshold V. In the parent, the target mapping changes with A only by a constant vertical shift of -A/membrane_capacitance, independent of V. In the child, adaptation exerts stronger suppression of dV_dt at larger depolarization because outward current scales with driving force, so the A effect is no longer a constant shift but a V-dependent interaction. This is scientifically admissible in sustained depolarization regimes where adaptation has recruited and the neuron remains below threshold. The contrast concerns the scored target mapping itself, not merely auxiliary A trajectories: even if observed A were held fixed, dV_dt would differ as a function of V. A single refit of existing parent parameters cannot reproduce this across conditions with equal A at different V because the parent's partial derivative with respect to A is fixed at -1/membrane_capacitance, whereas the child makes that slope vary with depolarization. Near rest or when A remains near zero, the refinement becomes hard to observe, which is an honest limitation."
  },
